# Caddy Reverse Proxy Configuration - Security Hardened
# Includes: HSTS, CSP, security headers, real rate limiting, auto-HTTPS
#
# Rate limiting uses the mholt/caddy-ratelimit plugin, compiled into the
# image via ./Dockerfile (the stock caddy image does not include it).
# {$DOMAIN} and {$QBIT_PORT} are substituted from the container's
# environment (see docker-compose.yml) at config-load time.
#
# Behind Cloudflare Tunnel (see the cloudflared service in
# docker-compose.yml), this server has no inbound port 80/443 access from
# the internet at all - Starlink's CGNAT makes that impossible anyway.
# acme_dns switches ALL certificate issuance from the old HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01
# challenges (which need an inbound request) to DNS-01 (proves domain
# ownership via a DNS TXT record instead) - this one global directive
# applies to every site block below with no further per-site changes,
# via the caddy-dns/cloudflare plugin compiled in by ./Dockerfile. The
# token needs Zone:DNS:Edit permission for this domain only - see
# SETUP.md for how to create it.

# Global settings
{
  email {$ACME_EMAIL}
  acme_ca https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
  acme_dns cloudflare {$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}

  order rate_limit after basic_auth
}

# Security header middleware (applied to all routes)
(security_headers) {
  # HTTPS Strict Transport Security (force HTTPS for 1 year)
  header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload"

  # Prevent MIME sniffing
  header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"

  # Prevent clickjacking
  header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"

  # XSS Protection (deprecated but good fallback)
  header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block"

  # Content Security Policy (prevent injection attacks)
  header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https:;"

  # Referrer Policy (privacy)
  header Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"

  # Permissions Policy (disable dangerous features)
  header Permissions-Policy "accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()"

  # Remove X-Powered-By (don't reveal tech stack)
  header -Server
}

# Rate limiting middleware (defense-in-depth against abuse/DoS - actual
# login brute-forcing is already stopped by basic_auth, which runs BEFORE
# this per the "order rate_limit after basic_auth" directive above, so a
# bad password never even reaches this counter).
# Each call site passes a unique zone name (import rate_limit <name>) so
# multiple site blocks don't collide on one shared zone definition.
# events was originally 30/min, which sounds generous until you remember
# these are all SPA dashboards: Sonarr/Radarr/Prowlarr/Lidarr each fire
# 15-30+ API calls just loading the status page, and qBittorrent/Seerr
# poll every few seconds for live queue/activity updates. Confirmed live
# via Caddy's own logs ("rate limit exceeded", zone sonarr_zone) that normal
# single-user browsing was tripping this within seconds of opening a tab,
# surfacing to the user as "Failed to load system status from API" -
# indistinguishable from the app actually being down. Raised well above
# realistic dashboard-polling volume while still bounding runaway abuse.
(rate_limit) {
  rate_limit {
    zone {args[0]}_zone {
      key    {remote_host}
      events 600
      window 1m
    }
  }
}

# Sonarr/Radarr/Prowlarr/Lidarr ship with authentication either disabled
# or defaulted to "disabled for local addresses" - a setting that becomes
# meaningless once traffic arrives via a reverse proxy, since every
# request now looks local to the app. Confirmed live via security audit:
# these were reachable with zero credentials from the public internet
# once the Cloudflare Tunnel wildcard rule made every subdomain reachable
# at all. One shared credential across all four (not a distinct one per
# app, the one deliberate exception to this repo's usual "never reuse a
# password" rule) since they're a single trust boundary in practice - the
# same admin (you) managing your own media automation, not a
# family-facing login. {$ARR_AUTH_HASH} is a bcrypt hash generated by
# gui-installer.ps1 via `caddy hash-password`, never a plaintext password.
(arr_auth) {
  basic_auth {
    {$ARR_AUTH_USER} {$ARR_AUTH_HASH}
  }
}

# Deliberately a separate login from arr_auth above, not reused: qBittorrent
# controls what actually gets downloaded, a worse outcome if this login
# leaks than reaching the Arr apps' library-management UI, so it isn't
# worth sharing one login across both risk levels.
(qbit_auth) {
  basic_auth {
    {$QBIT_AUTH_USER} {$QBIT_AUTH_HASH}
  }
}

# ========== MEDIA APPS ==========

# Sonarr - TV shows automation
sonarr.{$DOMAIN} {
  import security_headers
  import rate_limit sonarr
  import arr_auth
  reverse_proxy sonarr:8989
}

# Radarr - Movie automation
radarr.{$DOMAIN} {
  import security_headers
  import rate_limit radarr
  import arr_auth
  reverse_proxy radarr:7878
}

# Prowlarr - Indexer manager
prowlarr.{$DOMAIN} {
  import security_headers
  import rate_limit prowlarr
  import arr_auth
  reverse_proxy prowlarr:9696
}

# Lidarr - Music automation
lidarr.{$DOMAIN} {
  import security_headers
  import rate_limit lidarr
  import arr_auth
  reverse_proxy lidarr:8686
}

# qBittorrent - Torrent client WebUI (VPN-routed via gluetun's network namespace)
qbit.{$DOMAIN} {
  import security_headers
  import rate_limit qbit
  import qbit_auth
  reverse_proxy gluetun:{$QBIT_PORT} {
    header_up X-Real-IP {http.request.remote}
  }
}

# Seerr - lets family/friends request movies/shows without asking directly
requests.{$DOMAIN} {
  import security_headers
  import rate_limit requests
  reverse_proxy seerr:5055 {
    header_up X-Real-IP {http.request.remote}
  }
}

